A = {
30: """The most beautiful beach I've ever visited is Ky Co beach in Quy Nhon, in central Vietnam. I went there two years ago with my wife. To get there, we had to take a small boat from the city for about forty minutes, which was already an adventure. When we arrived, I was really amazed. The water was so clear that you could see the rocks and small fish at the bottom, and the color changed from light blue near the shore to deep blue farther out. The beach itself is quite small, and it's surrounded by green hills and big rocks, so it feels very private and peaceful. There weren't many tourists that day, which made it even better. My first impression was that it looked like a picture on a postcard. I still think it's the most beautiful place I've seen in Vietnam.""",
31: """I remember a trip to Cua Lo beach in Nghe An with my coworkers about three years ago. It was a company trip, and our whole team of around twenty people went there together by bus. We left very early on Saturday morning and arrived around noon. In the afternoon, we swam in the sea and played beach soccer, and everyone was laughing and shouting the whole time. In the evening, the company organized a big seafood barbecue right on the beach, and after dinner we had a bonfire and played games. What made this trip more memorable than others was that it was the first time I really got to know my coworkers outside of work. We talked about our families and our dreams, and I realized how much we had in common. Since that trip, our team has been much closer, and work feels more like being with friends.""",
32: """I really like traveling within Vietnam, because our country has so many different landscapes. In the north, my favorite place is Sa Pa. It's a mountain town where the weather is cool all year, and there are beautiful rice terraces and small villages of ethnic minority people. I love walking around there and breathing the fresh air. Another place I like is Da Nang in central Vietnam. It's a modern city, but it also has long, clean beaches and great seafood. From Da Nang, it's easy to visit Hoi An, an old town that's especially beautiful at night with all the colorful lanterns. I also enjoy Ha Long Bay, because taking a boat trip around the limestone islands is really relaxing. I like these places because they help me escape from my busy routine and spend quality time with my family.""",
33: """Well, I'm a pretty organized person, so I usually prepare for trips a week or two in advance. First, I talk with my family and decide where we want to go and for how many days. Then I look for hotels on a booking app and read the reviews carefully, because I want a clean place in a good location. After that, I book bus or plane tickets, depending on how far the place is. A few days before the trip, I check the weather forecast so I know what clothes to bring. For example, if we go to the mountains, I pack warm jackets, and if we go to the beach, I pack swimsuits and sunscreen. I also prepare a small bag with medicine, phone chargers, and snacks for my daughter. On the night before we leave, I double-check all our documents and money.""",
34: """An unforgettable trip for me was a family trip to Sa Pa about two years ago. It was in winter, and I went there with my wife, my daughter, and my parents. On the second day, we decided to drive up to a mountain pass to see the view. When we got there, it was incredibly foggy and freezing cold. We could barely see two meters ahead. Then, to our surprise, small pieces of ice started falling from the sky. It was actually snowing a little, which almost never happens in Vietnam. Everyone was so excited, especially my parents, who had never seen snow before in their lives. My father even started laughing like a child. We stood there for a long time taking photos, even though our hands were frozen. That moment made the trip really special, because the whole family shared something completely unexpected together.""",
35: """When I was young, my family didn't travel very often, because we didn't have much money. But every summer, we usually took one short trip. Most of the time, we went to the beach in Do Son, near Hai Phong, with my parents and my uncle's family. We traveled there by bus, and the trip took about three hours, which felt really long to me at that time. We usually stayed in a cheap guesthouse near the beach for two or three days. During the day, my cousins and I played in the sea for hours, and in the evening, the adults cooked seafood that they bought from local fishermen. I also remember a school trip to Hue when I was in high school. We visited the old citadel and the royal tombs, and that was the first time I became really interested in history.""",
36: """One of the countries I've visited is Thailand. I went to Bangkok and Pattaya with my wife about three years ago. Bangkok was really busy and modern, with lots of tall buildings, huge shopping malls, and a sky train running above the streets. But at the same time, there were beautiful temples everywhere with golden roofs, which was a nice mix of old and new. The traffic was terrible, though, even worse than in Hanoi. Pattaya was more relaxing, with long beaches and lots of seafood restaurants. As for the people, Thai people were very friendly and polite. They always smiled and greeted us with their hands together, which is a traditional Thai greeting. Many people in the tourist areas could speak some English, so it was easy to ask for directions. Overall, I had a great impression of Thailand, and I'd love to go back.""",
37: """My first trip to another country was to Singapore, about five years ago. It was actually a study tour organized by my company, so I went with three coworkers from my department. I was really excited, but also nervous, because it was my first time on a plane and I wasn't confident about my English. When we landed, the first thing I noticed was how clean and organized everything was. We stayed for four days. During the day, we visited a few factories and attended some training sessions. In the evenings, we went sightseeing at Marina Bay and Gardens by the Bay, which looked amazing at night with all the lights. We also tried local food at a hawker center, which is like a big outdoor food court. I tried chili crab for the first time, and it was delicious. That trip gave me the confidence to travel abroad again.""",
38: """Something unusual happened to me during a trip to Bangkok about three years ago. On the last day, my wife and I went to a famous weekend market, which is huge, with thousands of small shops. We were having so much fun shopping that we didn't notice the time. When we finally checked, we realized we only had two hours before our flight. We ran out to find a taxi, but it was rush hour, and the traffic was completely stuck. We were really panicking. Then a motorbike taxi driver saw us and offered to take us to the nearest sky train station. So my wife and I squeezed onto two motorbikes with our shopping bags, and we took the train to the airport. We arrived just thirty minutes before the gate closed. It was stressful at the time, but now it's a funny story we tell our friends.""",
39: """I think one of the most popular places for Vietnamese tourists abroad is South Korea, especially Seoul. There are several reasons why people like going there. First, many young Vietnamese people love Korean dramas and K-pop, so they want to visit the places they see on TV, like famous cafes, palaces, and shopping streets. Second, Korea has four distinct seasons, and many people want to see cherry blossoms in spring or snow in winter, because we don't have that in most of Vietnam. Third, Korean food, like barbecue and street food, is very popular here, so people are excited to try the real thing. Also, Seoul is a very safe and modern city with convenient public transportation. Finally, it's not too far from Vietnam, and there are many cheap flights now, so it's a good choice for a short holiday.""",
40: """When I was about fifteen, I visited Laos with my parents and my uncle's family. We traveled by car from Vietnam to Vientiane, the capital city. My first impression was that it was much quieter and more peaceful than Hanoi. The streets were wide and not very crowded, and there weren't many tall buildings. People seemed to live a slower life. I remember there were many beautiful Buddhist temples with golden roofs, and in the early morning, we saw monks in orange robes walking down the street to collect food from local people. That was really special for me. The food was a little different from Vietnamese food. We tried sticky rice eaten with our hands and a spicy papaya salad, which I liked a lot. Overall, I felt that Laos was a calm and friendly country, and the people were very kind to us.""",
41: """When I take a vacation at home, I mostly spend time with my family and close friends. Of course, I spend the most time with my wife and my daughter, because on normal workdays I'm usually too busy to play with my daughter for very long. I also visit my parents, who live about thirty minutes away in my hometown. My mother always cooks a big meal for us, and my father likes to chat with me about work and the news. Besides my family, I usually meet a few old friends from high school. One of them, Long, is my best friend. He's very funny and always organizes our get-togethers. We usually go out for coffee or have a small barbecue at someone's house. Spending time with these people helps me relax and recharge before going back to work.""",
42: """My last vacation at home was during the National Day holiday in early September. I had four days off. On the first day, I just stayed home and slept in, because I was really tired after a busy month at work. In the afternoon, I cleaned the apartment and fixed a few small things that I had been putting off for weeks. On the second day, we went back to my hometown to visit my parents. My mother cooked a big lunch, and my daughter played in the garden with her cousins all afternoon. On the third day, I met my high school friends for coffee in the morning, and in the evening we had a barbecue on my friend's rooftop. On the last day, my family went to the park and then watched a movie at home together. It was simple, but very relaxing.""",
43: """Something funny happened during my last Tet holiday at home. On the second day of Tet, my wife and I decided to make a big dinner for my parents and my in-laws. My wife was in charge of the main dishes, and I promised to make a cake for dessert, even though I had never baked anything before. I followed a video on YouTube very carefully, but I accidentally used salt instead of sugar, because the containers looked exactly the same. When everyone tasted the cake, they all made strange faces, and then my father-in-law burst out laughing. In the end, we threw the cake away and ate fruit instead, but everyone was laughing for the rest of the evening. It was memorable because it made the family dinner feel more relaxed and fun, and now my family teases me about it every Tet.""",
44: """The place I go to most often is a small craft beer bar near the city center. It's on a quiet street, about ten minutes from my house. From the outside, it looks like an old house, but inside it's cozy and modern. The lights are warm and a little dim, and there are wooden tables and chairs. On one wall, there's a big blackboard with the list of beers they have that day, and behind the counter you can see all the beer taps. They serve more than ten kinds of local craft beer, and also some simple food, like fries, sausages, and grilled squid. There's usually soft music playing, so it's easy to talk. What I like most about this bar is that it's not too noisy or crowded, and the staff are really friendly. It's a perfect place to relax with friends after work.""",
45: """I remember a funny incident at a bar about a year ago. I went to a sports bar with some coworkers to watch an important football match between Vietnam and Thailand. The bar was completely packed with fans wearing red shirts, and everyone was shouting and singing. In the last minute of the match, Vietnam scored the winning goal. The whole bar went crazy. People were jumping, hugging strangers, and banging on the tables. A man next to me got so excited that he accidentally knocked a full glass of beer onto my shirt. He apologized again and again, and then he insisted on buying beers for our whole table. We ended up chatting with him for the rest of the night, and it turned out he worked at a company near mine. It was special because of the amazing atmosphere, and I even made a new friend.""",
46: """Usually, when I want to get together with my friends at a pub, I message them in our group chat a few days before. We discuss which day works best for everyone, because we all have families and busy jobs now. Then one of us books a table, especially if it's a weekend, because the popular pubs get really crowded. I don't need to go to the bank before, because almost every place accepts QR code payments now, so I just bring my phone. When we arrive, we order a few rounds of beer and some snacks, like fried tofu, grilled pork, or spring rolls. Then we just chat about our work, our kids, and old memories from school. Sometimes we watch a football match if there's one on TV. At the end of the night, we usually split the bill, and we always take a taxi home.""",
47: """A special bar visit for me was my thirtieth birthday. My wife secretly planned a small party with my close friends at a rooftop bar in Hanoi. That afternoon, she told me we were just going out for a simple dinner. Before going to the bar, we had dinner at a Japanese restaurant nearby. Then she said she wanted to see the view from a rooftop. When we walked in, all of my best friends were there, holding a cake and shouting "Happy birthday!" I was so surprised and touched. We spent about three hours there. We drank cocktails, looked at the city lights, and my friends gave funny speeches about our university days. Later, we sang a few songs together with the live band. After the party, my friends and I went for some late-night street food. It's definitely my most memorable birthday so far.""",
48: """In Vietnam, there are many different kinds of restaurants, from small street food stalls to fancy restaurants in hotels. But the most typical restaurants are small family-run places. They usually have plastic or wooden tables and chairs, and sometimes they're open to the street, so you can see people cooking at the front. They're often busy, noisy, and full of people, especially at lunchtime. These restaurants usually focus on one or two dishes, like pho, bun cha, or broken rice. There are also many hot pot and barbecue restaurants, which are popular for groups of friends and families, because everyone can share food around one table. In bigger cities, you can also find a lot of international food, such as Korean, Japanese, and Italian. In general, restaurants in Vietnam are cheap, and the food is fresh and tasty.""",
49: """I recently went to a Korean barbecue restaurant near my house with my wife and some friends. It was last Friday evening, to celebrate my friend's promotion. The restaurant was quite big and modern, and every table had its own grill in the middle. Their menu had lots of different meats, like beef, pork belly, and marinated chicken, and you could also order side dishes, like kimchi, salad, and soup. We ordered a big set for five people, with three kinds of meat, and then we grilled the meat ourselves at the table. My favorite was the marinated beef, because it was really tender and juicy. We also had some cold drinks and a bowl of seafood soup at the end. I really liked how the food tasted, and the atmosphere was fun, because everyone was cooking and chatting together.""",
50: """The last time I used a delivery service was last Tuesday. I had to work late that day, and my wife was also very tired, so we decided not to cook. I opened a food delivery app on my phone and ordered from a pizza restaurant near my house. We got a large seafood pizza, a small salad, and some fried chicken for my daughter. The order arrived in about thirty minutes, which was faster than I expected. The driver even called me when he was downstairs, so I didn't have to wait. The pizza was still hot, and it tasted pretty good, although it was a bit greasy. What I remember most is that my daughter was so happy, because she rarely gets to eat pizza on a weekday. It was a nice and easy dinner after a long day.""",
51: """For my mother's sixtieth birthday last year, we decided to have a family party at her house. Since there were about fifteen people, we didn't want her to spend the whole day cooking, so I ordered most of the food from a restaurant that offers party delivery. A few days before, I called them and chose a set menu. It included roast duck, steamed chicken, spring rolls, a seafood salad, and a big pot of hot pot. On the day of the party, the food arrived on time, and it was nicely packed in trays, so we only had to warm some dishes up. I also ordered a birthday cake from a bakery nearby. Everyone said the food was delicious, and my mother was really happy, because for once she could just sit and enjoy the party with her grandchildren instead of cooking in the kitchen.""",
52: """In my community, there are coffee shops everywhere, almost on every street. Coffee is a big part of Vietnamese culture, so people of all ages go to cafes. There are a few different types. The first type is small, traditional cafes, where people sit on low plastic stools on the sidewalk and drink strong Vietnamese coffee with condensed milk. These are usually full of older men in the morning. The second type is modern cafes, which are more popular with young people. They usually have nice decorations, air conditioning, and free Wi-Fi, so students often go there to study or work on their laptops. There are also a few big coffee chains in shopping malls. My favorite one is a small cafe near my house with lots of plants and wooden furniture. It's quiet, and the coffee is cheap but really good.""",
53: """When I go to coffee shops, I usually go with my friends or coworkers. On weekend mornings, I often meet my old friends at a cafe near my house. We order iced milk coffee and just chat about work, family, and football for a couple of hours. Sometimes I also go to a cafe to work, especially when I need to prepare a report and the house is too noisy because my daughter is playing. I bring my laptop, find a quiet corner, and put on my headphones. At work, my team sometimes has small meetings at the coffee shop inside our company, because it's more relaxed than the meeting room. What I like most about going to coffee shops is the relaxed atmosphere. It's a nice place to take a break from my busy life and just enjoy a cup of coffee.""",
54: """I remember a funny incident at a coffee shop about two years ago. I was meeting a business partner for the first time at a cafe near my office. We had only talked on the phone, so I didn't know what he looked like. He said he was wearing a blue shirt, so when I arrived, I walked up to a man in a blue shirt, shook his hand, and started introducing my company. The man looked very confused but listened politely for a few minutes. Then my phone rang, and it was my real business partner, who was sitting at the next table, also wearing a blue shirt, and laughing at me. I was so embarrassed. I apologized to the stranger, and luckily he was very friendly about it. In the end, the three of us laughed together, and it actually helped break the ice for our meeting.""",
55: """The first coffee shop I ever went to was a small cafe near my high school. I was in tenth grade, and before that, I thought coffee was only for adults. One afternoon after class, my classmates invited me to go there, and I felt very grown-up. The cafe was in an old house, and it was quite dark inside, with old wooden tables and loud pop music. I remember there was a big fish tank near the door and lots of posters of singers on the walls. I ordered a glass of iced milk coffee, and it was so strong that I couldn't sleep that night. What I remember most is the feeling of hanging out with my friends there. After that, we went there almost every week to chat and do homework together. The cafe closed a few years ago, but I still have great memories of it.""",
56: """In Vietnam, people love getting together to celebrate, and family is usually at the center of every celebration. The biggest one is Tet, the Lunar New Year, when everyone goes back to their hometown to be with their families. People cook traditional food, visit relatives, and give lucky money to children. Besides Tet, people also celebrate weddings, birthdays, and death anniversaries of their ancestors. Weddings are especially big. Sometimes there are hundreds of guests, and the party is held in a big restaurant or a tent in front of the house. When people get together, they usually eat a lot, drink beer, and toast many times while shouting "one, two, three, cheers!" At many parties, people also sing karaoke. In general, celebrations in Vietnam are noisy, warm, and full of food.""",
57: """My last celebration was my daughter's fifth birthday about two weeks ago. We had a small party at our apartment on Saturday afternoon. I invited my parents, my in-laws, and some of my daughter's friends from kindergarten with their parents. In the morning, my wife and I decorated the living room with balloons and a big banner that said "Happy Birthday." We also ordered a cake with her favorite cartoon character on it. My wife cooked some simple food, like fried chicken, spring rolls, and fruit salad. When the guests arrived, the kids played games together while the adults chatted. Then we sang "Happy Birthday" and my daughter blew out the candles. She was so excited when she opened her presents, especially a new bicycle from her grandparents. It was a busy day, but seeing her so happy made it worth it.""",
58: """I remember something memorable that happened at my cousin's wedding a few years ago. The wedding party was held in a big tent in front of my uncle's house in the countryside, with more than two hundred guests. Everything was going well until, in the middle of the party, a sudden storm hit. Strong wind started blowing the tent, and heavy rain poured in from one side. Everyone had to grab their plates and run into the house. The bride's dress got wet, and some of the decorations were ruined. But instead of being upset, the bride and groom just laughed. Then the guests started singing and dancing inside the house, and it turned into a really fun party. It was unforgettable because something that could have ruined the wedding actually made it more special and brought everyone closer.""",
59: """In my area, people usually have celebrations in three kinds of places. For small family celebrations, like birthdays or death anniversaries, people usually gather at someone's home, often the oldest family member's house. For bigger events like weddings, most people in the city book a party restaurant or a wedding hall. Let me describe a typical wedding hall near my house. It's a large building with a huge room that can hold around five hundred people. Inside, there are many round tables covered with white tablecloths, and at the front there's a big stage with bright lights and a sound system, where the couple stands and where people sing. The decorations are usually flowers and colorful lights. In the countryside, people still often set up a big tent in front of their house instead. Sometimes young people also have parties at rooftop bars or barbecue restaurants.""",
}
